{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\": \"R-25:62\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Both verses join repeated practice with remembrance or gratitude: 25:62 names alternating night and day as an occasion for remembrance or thanks, while 5:6 ends with hoped-for thanks.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"25:62\", \"ar\": \"جَعَلَ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ خِلْفَةًۭ لِّمَنْ أَرَادَ أَن يَذَّكَّرَ أَوْ أَرَادَ شُكُورًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s closing gratitude clause follows a recurring prayer condition.\", \"limits\": \"25:62 does not mention prayer or purification, so it does not identify the rite as the remembrance intended there.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-26:49\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The same paired limbs appear in opposite bodily actions: 5:6 directs washing and wiping hands and feet, while Pharaoh threatens to cut them crosswise. This contrast can make the focus’s treatment of the limbs audible as care rather than coercion.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ وَأَيْدِيَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْمَرَافِقِ وَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِرُءُوسِكُمْ وَأَرْجُلَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْكَعْبَيْنِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"26:49\", \"ar\": \"لَأُقَطِّعَنَّ أَيْدِيَكُمْ وَأَرْجُلَكُم مِّنْ خِلَٰفٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact pairing أيديكم وأرجلكم occurs in both passages, with cleansing in one and threatened severing in the other.\", \"limits\": \"The wording establishes a bodily contrast, not a deliberate allusion between the passages.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-26:73\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-26:80\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus includes illness among the conditions for an adjusted purification practice; Abraham’s words link illness with God’s healing.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ\"}, {\"ref\": \"26:80\", \"ar\": \"وَإِذَا مَرِضْتُ فَهُوَ يَشْفِينِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages name illness, and the focus places it within a provision for worship.\", \"limits\": \"26:80 speaks of healing illness; it does not say purification heals the sick.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-26:122\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-27:19\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Both passages connect divine favor with gratitude. Solomon asks for help to thank God for a named favor and to do righteous work; 5:6 presents purification and completed favor as aims leading to communal gratitude.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"27:19\", \"ar\": \"رَبِّ أَوْزِعْنِىٓ أَنْ أَشْكُرَ نِعْمَتَكَ ٱلَّتِىٓ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَىَّ وَعَلَىٰ وَٰلِدَىَّ وَأَنْ أَعْمَلَ صَٰلِحًۭا تَرْضَىٰهُ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus pairs نعمة and تشكرون; Solomon’s prayer pairs نعمتك and أشكر with righteous action.\", \"limits\": \"Solomon asks for capacity to respond, whereas 5:6 states a hoped-for outcome for its addressees; the verses do not equate the rite itself with gratitude.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-27:55\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ may be heard beside a passage that explicitly frames men’s approach to women in terms of sexual desire and then describes Lot’s household as people who purify themselves. This scene may bear on the range of contact and purification in 5:6.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ\"}, {\"ref\": \"27:55\", \"ar\": \"أَئِنَّكُمْ لَتَأْتُونَ ٱلرِّجَالَ شَهْوَةًۭ مِّن دُونِ ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"27:56\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّهُمْ أُنَاسٌۭ يَتَطَهَّرُونَ\"}], \"missing\": \"This scene supplies a sexual setting and a purity term, but not a direct parallel for لامستم. Quranic usage or a dictionary sense showing whether لمس النساء can denote intercourse rather than ordinary contact could decide how much it bears on the focus.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-27:58\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-27:73\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus hopes that its addressees will give thanks for divine favor; 27:73 states that most people do not give thanks despite their Lord’s favor. The parallel makes the focus’s لَعَلَّكُمْ تشكرون an invited response, not a guaranteed one.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"27:73\", \"ar\": \"وَإِنَّ رَبَّكَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى ٱلنَّاسِ وَلَٰكِنَّ أَكْثَرَهُمْ لَا يَشْكُرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both verses pair divine favor with the response of thanks, but 27:73 supplies the contrary outcome.\", \"limits\": \"27:73 speaks broadly of people and favor; it does not refer specifically to ritual purification.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-30:46\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Both verses end a sequence of divine provisions or purposes with لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ. In 30:46 the sequence includes winds, mercy, ships, and seeking favor; in 5:6 it follows purification and completed favor.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"30:46\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيَذِيقَكُم مِّن رَّحْمَتِهِۦ وَلِتَجْرِىَ ٱلْفُلْكُ بِأَمْرِهِۦ وَلِتَبْتَغُوا۟ مِن فَضْلِهِۦ وَلَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared closing formula places the focus’s rite among divine provisions that invite gratitude.\", \"limits\": \"30:46 concerns natural signs and provision at sea; it does not interpret the purification procedure.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-32:8\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:3\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:7\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:23\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:24\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:33\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Both verses state divine purification as a purpose with the same verb and object pronoun: يُطَهِّرَكُمْ. This makes the focus’s ritual instructions part of a wider Quranic language for divine purifying action.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:33\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّمَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيُذْهِبَ عَنكُمُ ٱلرِّجْسَ أَهْلَ ٱلْبَيْتِ وَيُطَهِّرَكُمْ تَطْهِيرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact verb يُطَهِّرَكُمْ appears in both, and 33:33 places it alongside removal of الرجس.\", \"limits\": \"The addressees and settings differ; 33:33 does not equate its purification with the focus’s ritual procedure.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:37\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Both passages frame a divine ruling through the word حَرَج. In 33:37, a rule about marriage removes constriction from believers; in 5:6, ritual rules and an alternative are explained by the denial of intended constriction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:37\", \"ar\": \"لِكَىْ لَا يَكُونَ عَلَى ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ حَرَجٌۭ فِىٓ أَزْوَٰجِ أَدْعِيَآئِهِمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared term حرج occurs in legal contexts, with a rule and its scope stated in each passage.\", \"limits\": \"The subject of the rule differs: marriage in 33:37, ritual purification in 5:6.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:38\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The phrase حَرَجٍۢ is used again for what God has prescribed, here for the Prophet. This gives the focus’s no-constriction statement a nearby Quranic legal usage while showing that its scope must be read with each ruling.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:38\", \"ar\": \"مَّا كَانَ عَلَى ٱلنَّبِىِّ مِنْ حَرَجٍۢ فِيمَا فَرَضَ ٱللَّهُ لَهُۥ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same hardship term appears in a statement about divinely prescribed matters.\", \"limits\": \"33:38 concerns the Prophet and a distinct ruling; it does not describe a general alternative for believers.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:40\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:50\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s denial of intended hardship has a close legal parallel in 33:50, where a marriage provision is given so that there be no constriction upon the Prophet.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:50\", \"ar\": \"لِكَيْلَا يَكُونَ عَلَيْكَ حَرَجٌۭ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use a purpose construction with حرج to explain a divine ruling.\", \"limits\": \"The focus addresses believers’ purification; 33:50 concerns a provision specific to the Prophet.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:53\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus links contact with women to purification, while 33:53 calls a boundary around interaction with the Prophet’s wives أَطْهَرُ لِقُلُوبِكُمْ. Together they show bodily purification and relational or inward purity using the same root.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:53\", \"ar\": \"ذَٰلِكُمْ أَطْهَرُ لِقُلُوبِكُمْ وَقُلُوبِهِنَّ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus names women and the root طهر; 33:53 names women’s interaction and uses the same root for hearts.\", \"limits\": \"The verses describe different kinds of purity, and this parallel does not settle whether لامستم means ordinary touch or intercourse.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-33:66\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The focus repeatedly names faces as surfaces to wash or wipe; 33:66 presents faces as a visible sign in the afterlife. The shared body-part vocabulary may connect ritual preparation with later bodily outcome.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِوُجُوهِكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"33:66\", \"ar\": \"يَوْمَ تُقَلَّبُ وُجُوهُهُمْ فِى ٱلنَّارِ\"}], \"missing\": \"The verses share the face as a bodily site but do not link washing to judgment. Another Quranic passage explicitly connecting ritual purification with eschatological faces could establish the relationship.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-34:13\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The command to David’s family, اعْمَلُوا شُكْرًا, casts gratitude as something enacted. After a sequence of bodily commands, 5:6 likewise presents gratitude as the intended response to divine favor.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"34:13\", \"ar\": \"ٱعْمَلُوٓا۟ ءَالَ دَاوُۥدَ شُكْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus places gratitude after embodied practice; 34:13 directly joins action and gratitude.\", \"limits\": \"34:13 does not identify any particular act of worship as gratitude, so it does not label the purification rite itself as shukr.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-34:15\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus calls the selected earth طَيِّبًا; 34:15 describes a fertile land as طَيِّبَةٌ and places it beside provision and a command to give thanks. The shared adjective links the rite’s medium with Quranic language for a good and sustaining place.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"34:15\", \"ar\": \"كُلُوا۟ مِن رِّزْقِ رَبِّكُمْ وَٱشْكُرُوا۟ لَهُۥ ۚ بَلْدَةٌۭ طَيِّبَةٌۭ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact adjective طيب describes both the selected surface and the land associated with provision and gratitude.\", \"limits\": \"The shared adjective does not make the two substances identical or say that the soil itself performs purification.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-35:37\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-36:20\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-37:65\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-37:98\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Both passages pair intention and making: Abraham’s opponents intend a plot and God makes them lowest, while 5:6 contrasts what God does not intend to make with what He does intend. The pairing makes agency and outcome explicit, though their aims differ sharply.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"37:98\", \"ar\": \"فَأَرَادُوا۟ بِهِۦ كَيْدًۭا فَجَعَلْنَٰهُمُ ٱلْأَسْفَلِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The passages use the roots ر و د and ج ع ل to connect intention with an outcome.\", \"limits\": \"The shared roots do not establish a specific allusion; 37:98 concerns a thwarted human plot, not a legal provision.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-38:3\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-38:33\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The Quran also uses مَسْحًا for an action directed at shins and necks in a narrative outside ritual law. This keeps the focus’s امسحوا open as a bodily contact verb whose exact effect and scope must come from its construction and context.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِرُءُوسِكُمْ وَأَرْجُلَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"38:33\", \"ar\": \"فَطَفِقَ مَسْحًۢا بِٱلسُّوقِ وَٱلْأَعْنَاقِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use the root م س ح with body parts as the object or target of the action.\", \"limits\": \"The passage does not settle whether the action in 38:33 is wiping, stroking, or something else, and it does not decide the focus’s foot construction.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-38:42\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Job’s recovery scene brings illness, a foot, and a cool bathing place together. Alongside 5:6’s illness provision, foot reference, and purification media, it allows the focus’s procedure to be heard within a Quranic scene of bodily restoration.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَأَرْجُلَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْكَعْبَيْنِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ\"}, {\"ref\": \"38:42\", \"ar\": \"ٱرْكُضْ بِرِجْلِكَ ۖ هَٰذَا مُغْتَسَلٌۢ بَارِدٌۭ وَشَرَابٌۭ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus names illness and feet in a purification instruction; Job’s verse joins a foot to washing and drinking after illness.\", \"limits\": \"Job’s bathing place is not a ritual purification rule, and the connection does not make the focus a cure for illness.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-39:45\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-39:60\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The focus makes the face a repeated surface of ritual washing or wiping; 39:60 makes faces a visible sign at judgment. The shared image could add an eschatological dimension to bodily preparation.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِوُجُوهِكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"39:60\", \"ar\": \"تَرَى ٱلَّذِينَ كَذَبُوا۟ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ وُجُوهُهُم مُّسْوَدَّةٌ\"}], \"missing\": \"The verses share face imagery but do not connect washing with the judgment sign. A Quranic passage that explicitly links ritual face-purification to the fate or appearance of faces at judgment could supply that bridge.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-39:66\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"39:66 commands the addressee to worship God and be among the grateful; 5:6 ends with gratitude as the hoped-for response to purification and favor.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"39:66\", \"ar\": \"بَلِ ٱللَّهَ فَٱعْبُدْ وَكُن مِّنَ ٱلشَّٰكِرِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages connect worship of God with human gratitude.\", \"limits\": \"39:66 does not identify purification as the means or occasion for gratitude.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-39:73\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus calls the chosen surface طَيِّبًا; at the garden’s entrance, its keepers say طِبْتُمْ. This shared root lets the material quality named for the purification medium sit alongside Quranic language of goodness or purity associated with the saved.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"39:73\", \"ar\": \"سَلَٰمٌ عَلَيْكُمْ طِبْتُمْ فَٱدْخُلُوهَا خَٰلِدِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The rare root ط ي ب links the medium in the focus to the welcome at the garden’s gate.\", \"limits\": \"The greeting does not say that the rite confers entry or that طيب has an identical sense in both verses.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-41:51\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus presents gratitude as a hoped-for response to favor; 41:51 describes a contrary human response, turning away when favor is granted.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"41:51\", \"ar\": \"وَإِذَآ أَنْعَمْنَا عَلَى ٱلْإِنسَٰنِ أَعْرَضَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages join divine favor with the human response to it.\", \"limits\": \"41:51 describes a general pattern and does not refer to ritual law.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-42:23\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus asks people to give thanks; 42:23 calls God شَكُور after describing an increase in the good earned by a person. The shared root sets human gratitude alongside God’s responsive rewarding.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"42:23\", \"ar\": \"وَمَن يَقْتَرِفْ حَسَنَةًۭ نَّزِدْ لَهُۥ فِيهَا حُسْنًا ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ شَكُورٌ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same root ش ك ر describes the human response in 5:6 and a divine attribute in 42:23.\", \"limits\": \"42:23 does not mention purification or say that gratitude is a payment for the rite.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-42:33\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"42:33 names patience and gratitude as responses to changing conditions at sea. The focus likewise closes with gratitude after provision for differing conditions, including illness, travel, and absence of water.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"42:33\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍۢ لِّكُلِّ صَبَّارٍۢ شَكُورٍ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages associate gratitude with divine provision amid changing conditions.\", \"limits\": \"42:33 concerns ships and wind, not purification.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-43:17\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-43:33\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-43:57\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"X1\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus echoes 5:3’s completion-of-favor formula: أَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِى is followed here by وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ. This places ritual purification within the surah’s language of divine favor and its completion.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:3\", \"ar\": \"وَأَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِى\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The verses share the root ت م م, نعمتي/نعمته, and the phrase عَلَيْكُمْ.\", \"limits\": \"The verbal forms differ, and the echo does not by itself establish a chronology or that each verse names the same specific favor.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X2\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The next verse tells the same believers to remember God’s favor and covenant, recalling their words سَمِعْنَا وَأَطَعْنَا. Following 5:6’s purification rule and hoped-for gratitude, this places the rite inside a movement from favor to grateful, obedient remembrance.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:7\", \"ar\": \"وَٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَمِيثَٰقَهُ ٱلَّذِى وَاثَقَكُم بِهِۦٓ إِذْ قُلْتُمْ سَمِعْنَا وَأَطَعْنَا\"}], \"activation\": \"The immediately following verse repeats the favor upon you formula and supplies a remembered response of obedience.\", \"limits\": \"5:7 does not name purification as the covenant’s content or state that this particular rite fulfills it.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X3\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Later in the same surah, hands and feet appear again in a legal ruling, this time as body parts threatened with crosswise cutting. Against that passage, 5:6’s detailed washing and wiping can be heard as another legal treatment of the same limbs, directed toward worship rather than punishment.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ وَأَيْدِيَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْمَرَافِقِ وَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِرُءُوسِكُمْ وَأَرْجُلَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْكَعْبَيْنِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:33\", \"ar\": \"أَوْ تُقَطَّعَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَأَرْجُلُهُم مِّنْ خِلَٰفٍ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same surah names أيديكم وأرجلكم in both passages, first in a purification procedure and later in a punishment.\", \"limits\": \"The contrast is grounded in shared wording and legal context; the text does not state that the two rulings are directly paired.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X4\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The verse immediately before the focus discusses lawful marriage and women, then 5:6 names لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ as a condition in a purification rule. Their proximity could activate a sexual sense of لمس in the focus.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"5:5\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلْمُحْصَنَٰتُ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنَٰتِ وَٱلْمُحْصَنَٰتُ مِنَ ٱلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ مِن قَبْلِكُمْ إِذَآ ءَاتَيْتُمُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ مُحْصِنِينَ غَيْرَ مُسَٰفِحِينَ وَلَا مُتَّخِذِىٓ أَخْدَانٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"5:6\", \"ar\": \"أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ\"}], \"missing\": \"The adjacency supplies a marriage and sexual-morality setting but does not decide the lexical sense of لامستم. A dictionary sense or Quranic usage parallel connecting this form to intercourse rather than ordinary contact could resolve the reading.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}"}}
